      "When the fictional town of Bellwick opened a seed library, its organisers assumed that a catalogue would be their most useful contribution. They collected packets from experienced gardeners, labelled the varieties and placed them in drawers beside the public library desk. Visitors could take seeds without payment and were encouraged, though never required, to return seeds from a later harvest. The first season brought considerable interest. Yet the librarians soon noticed that the varieties most often borrowed were not necessarily the ones most often returned. A system designed to circulate living material needed more than an inventory.",
      "The original labels recorded a variety's name and the year in which its seeds had been harvested. Gardeners asked different questions: would the beans tolerate a windy plot, and would a tomato ripen before the first cold nights? The library added a field for growing conditions, keeping these observations separate from verified variety names. An account from one garden could be helpful without being a universal recommendation. When two accounts disagreed, both remained visible. Staff did not average away differences that might reflect meaningful variation in soil, shelter or the timing of planting.",
