      "A fictional field course at Pinewater Reserve asked beginners to identify birds from brief observations. At first, the tutors supplied illustrated sheets arranged by species name. Learners could recognise familiar pictures indoors but struggled when a moving bird appeared against a bright sky. The course team suspected that recognition in a worksheet and identification in the field were related but different tasks. They redesigned the course around what could actually be observed in uncertain conditions, rather than treating a failure to name a species as evidence that a learner had not studied hard enough.",
