      "In the fictional valley of Hartmere, flood management had long concentrated on keeping water inside a narrow channel. Walls were raised after damaging floods, and bends were straightened to carry water away more quickly. These works protected particular sites, but downstream residents continued to experience sudden rises. Engineers began investigating whether moving water faster through one reach could contribute to difficulties elsewhere. The question did not imply that all existing defences should be removed. It asked whether a catchment-wide strategy needed to consider the places and times at which water could safely spread out.",
