      "Computer models compared several arrangements. Each model required assumptions about rainfall, soil saturation, channel roughness and the operation of existing structures. The engineers tested a range of plausible values instead of reporting only the most favourable simulation. Maps showed depths and durations, not merely the boundary of a coloured flood area. For farmers, a shallow flood lasting a day could have different consequences from standing water lasting a week. Presenting that distinction helped residents understand the proposal and exposed assumptions that would otherwise remain hidden inside a single map.",
